  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A frontal zone stalled just south of the area will \nbring another period of showers and isolated thunderstorms through \nthe first half of today. \n\nThis morning a stalled frontal boundary lies near the state line, \nwith convection across OH/PA. Expect this convection to expand \nnorthward across WNY this morning, within a moisture rich \nenvironment within an inverted surface trough. Thunderstorm \ndevelopment will be limited as MUCAPE values generally lie below 500 \nJ/KG.  However, as Pwats become greater than 1.75 inches to around \n2.00 inches and near the 90th percentile or even possible record \nvalues per Buffalo sounding climatology for this time of year there \nis still the potential for localized ponding of water in heavier or \ntraining activity. Greatest risk is likely across the Southern Tier, \nlying closer to the frontal boundary and low level convergence. \n\nThe timing of convection, generally in the morning, combined with \nweak instability will lend to a low severe threat, though the \nmoisture rich environment could pose a hydrologic issue with heavier \nstorms, potentially passing over the same areas this morning.\n \nKEY MESSAGE 2...A brief period of drier, cooler conditions is \nexpected tonight through Saturday morning. \n\nThe frontal zone will accelerate southeast and away from the region \ntonight as high pressure builds southeast across Ontario and Quebec. \nDendritic river valley fog is likely to form across the Southern \nTier and Black River valleys during the overnight hours. It may even \nbecome cold enough Friday night, that if winds become calm, that \nisolated patches of light frost form in the traditionally colder \nareas of the inland North Country. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Unsettled pattern this weekend and into the \nbeginning of next week. \n\nAn unsettled pattern emerges Saturday as a warm front lifts back \nnorthward across our region as surface high pressure exits to the \neast. Several additional shortwave troughs will ripple across our \nregion through the weekend and into the beginning of next week \nmaintaining chances for showers and perhaps a few thunderstorms. \nEnsembles data from the GEFS, GEPS and ENS continue to depict \nSaturday night into Sunday morning with the greatest chances for \nprecipitation, this as a weak surface low expands northeastward from \nthe Ohio Valley along with developing weak instability. Several \nadditional shortwave troughs and the eventual passage of an open \nwave trough (currently featured as a closed low over NorCal) will \nmaintain chances for showers through the first half of the new work \nweek.",
